From 0a2da577520668a5d6059d4253ba8f53c6c05963 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:17:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Apply pod network sysctls (linux.sysctl) inside a new vnet
 jail

CRI-O passes a pod's sysctls (from securityContext.sysctls and the
default_sysctls config) in the sandbox spec's linux.sysctl, which ocijail
ignored. When ocijail creates a jail with its own vnet (the pod sandbox),
translate the entries that have a per-vnet FreeBSD equivalent and set them
from inside the jail (fork, jail_attach, sysctlbyname; the kernel lets a
jail that owns its vnet write CTLFLAG_VNET sysctls):

  net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=N -> net.inet.ip.portrange.reservedhigh=max(N-1,0)
  net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range="LO HI"  -> net.inet.ip.portrange.first/last

Others are logged and ignored. With CRI-O's default_sysctls set to
net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=0 (the containerd 2.x default), non-root
processes can bind ports below 1024 in their pod, e.g. ingress-nginx
(uid 101 + NET_BIND_SERVICE), while the host keeps its reserved ports.
Translation is unit tested (sysctl_test).
---
